First, we will create one or more unified kernel image(s) (UKI).

Typically, the boot loader will execute what is called an EFI boot stub to start
Linux. This is a kernel image packaged as a UEFI executable. Arguments can be
passed to the kernel via the commandline, which can be set either directly in
the boot loader or in its on-disk configuration. The EFI stub then loads an
environment containing all the necessary programs, drivers and configuration
files required to bootstrap the system. This environment is referred to either
as an initial RAM file system (initramfs) or an initial RAM disk (initrd). Both
terms are often used interchangeably or with subtle differentiation.

A unified kernel image packages the kernel, initramfs and commandline parameters
into a single EFI executable. This makes the boot process more manageable and
also allows usage of features like Secure Boot as it reduces the number of files
involved in the early boot stage dramatically.

To create a UKI, we will adapt the configuration and preset files for
mkinitcpio and write our kernel commandline to a designated file. This script
should take care of most of the work but each config file will be opened with a
text editor ($EDITOR) so you can make custom adjustments.

One thing to note is that we will be using a systemd based initramfs. Arch Linux
by default uses busybox but using systemd gives us access to a couple of handy
extensions during the boot process.
